license: MIT
keywords:
  - ai-safety
  - alignment
  - evaluation-awareness
  - llm-evaluation
  - sandbagging
  - reasoning-models
abstract: >
  Empirical alignment-eval research and infrastructure. V1 (complete) measures
  whether explicit "you are being evaluated" framing changes refusal behavior
  on eight frontier models and whether the effect is mediated by visible
  verbalized eval-awareness in chain-of-thought. Pre-registered disjoint-prompt
  replication of the qwen3 safety mediation effect returned null; what survives
  is a direction-asymmetric refusal shift (qwen3 +22pp, opus-4.7 -34pp) that
  operates on prompts where neither model verbalized the evaluation. V2
  extends the analysis using Apollo Research's released eval-awareness corpus.
